"""Hope CNC — modern CNC machine control, as an internal Hope tool (docs/CNC_HOPE.md).

A Mach3 replacement designed in two layers:

* **PC (this package, Python):** interprets the G-code (including Mach3 habits), plans the motion
  with lookahead and per-axis velocity and acceleration limits, checks the program against the
  machine (travel limits, spindle, tools) and translates it into the controller's dialect.
* **Real time (outside Python):** step pulses are generated by a dedicated controller
  (grblHAL/FluidNC over USB or Ethernet) or by LinuxCNC (parallel port on old PCs, Mesa cards on
  modern ones). Python does not generate pulses: it is not real-time.

Modules:

* ``machine`` — machine configuration (YAML) and coordinate systems.
* ``gcode`` — interpreter: G-code → operations (moves, arcs, dwells, spindle…).
* ``planner`` — lookahead planner (grbl-style junction deviation) and exact timings.
* ``check`` — checks the program against the machine; text report.
* ``dialect`` — translation to grbl/grblHAL or LinuxCNC (expanded canned cycles, manual M6, G43).
* ``backends`` — simulator, grbl/grblHAL (serial) and LinuxCNC.
* ``controller`` — ties everything together; **real motion goes through the guardian**
  (``hardware.control``, human level): Hope can check and simulate, but never move the machine on
  its own.
* ``medir`` — PC performance benchmark (``python -m hope.cnc medir``).
* ``session``, ``links``, ``profiles``, ``app`` — the Mach3-style screen (TISMA CNC): work session,
  live link with the controller, machine profiles and local web server.
* ``__main__`` — the console: ``python -m hope.cnc revisar|traducir|simular|estado|enviar|app|medir``.

Path of a program
-----------------
::

    G-code text ──gcode.interpret──▶ Program ──check.check_program──▶ report (runnable?)
                                         │                                  │ (planner.plan: timings)
                                         └──dialect.emit──▶ normalized G-code ──backend.stream──▶ machine
                                                                 (with human approval if it is real)

Units and conventions (throughout the package)
----------------------------------------------
* mm, mm/min for F (as in G-code), mm/s and mm/s² inside the planner, seconds for times.
* **Machine** coordinates internally; work coordinates (G54…) only when displaying and translating.
* Python 3.7+ (Windows Vista) and translatable to 3.4 (Windows XP): see the repository's ``CLAUDE.md``.

Usage example
-------------
::

    from hope.cnc.check import check_text, report_text
    from hope.cnc.machine import load_machine

    m = load_machine("configs/cnc_maquina.yaml")
    print(report_text(check_text(open("pieza.nc").read(), m)))
"""
